<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Camilo Henríquez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1769">1769</a>–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Camilo Henríquez was a Chilean priest, writer and patriot of the independence era. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdivia">Valdivia</a> and educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, he edited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurora-de-chile">Aurora de Chile</a>, the country's first newspaper, from 1812, using it to promote independence and republican ideas. After the royalist victory of 1814 he went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> in 1822. He is regarded as the father of Chilean journalism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/camilo-henriquez.jpg" alt="Camilo Henríquez" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CamiloHenr%C3%ADquez.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henríquez was born on 29 July 1769 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdivia">Valdivia</a>, in a house belonging to his grandmother Margarita de Castro; his father, Félix Henríquez y Santillán, had been a Spanish infantry captain. At nine he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> to study at the Convictorio Carolino, and in 1784, at fifteen, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> under his maternal uncle Juan Nepomuceno González, a member of the order known as the Buena Muerte. Taught there by Friar Isidoro de Celis, a strong proponent of science and rationality, he joined the order as a novice in 1787 and was ordained a priest on 28 January 1790.</p><p>Influenced by the French Enlightenment, he read books banned by the Spanish government, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a>'s Social Contract. In 1809 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a> arrested and interrogated him; he never explained why, and the reading of forbidden books is only the most commonly cited reason. After his release his order sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> to found a convent, where he witnessed royalist violence that he later drew on in his play Camila o la Patriota de Sud América.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> in 1811, he wrote the Proclama de Quirino Lemachez under a pseudonym formed from an anagram of his name, urging support for pro-independence candidates for the First National Congress. Drawing on the social contract theorists, it argued that since the patriots had never consented to a pact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, they should establish their own government; it was reprinted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He sat in the Congress as interim deputy for Puchacay and became president of the senate in 1813. The first issue of La Aurora de Chile appeared on 13 February 1812, and its successor, the Monitor Araucano, on 17 April 1813.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rancagua">Disaster of Rancagua</a>, where his brother José Manuel was killed defending one of the trenches of the town's plaza, Henríquez fled to Mendoza and later escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. There he contributed to La Gaceta de Buenos Aires and El Censor, and he reportedly studied mathematics and medicine while in exile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He came back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins">Bernardo O'Higgins</a>, became librarian of the National Library of Chile and edited the Gazeta Ministerial de Chile. He was a deputy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiloe">Chiloé</a> in 1823 and for Copiapó in 1824, and sat on the nine-member conservative senate that advised the Supreme Director, Ramón Freire. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> on 16 March 1825, and the government declared a national period of mourning. In all he edited at least ten newspapers in Santiago and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Camilo Henríquez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>José Miguel Carrera seized power in a series of coups and introduced a provisional constitution, the first national flag and the newspaper Aurora de Chile, edited by <b>Camilo Henríquez</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/patria-vieja">Patria Vieja</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdivia">Valdivia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-39.81,-73.25&amp;year=1805" aria-label="Valdivia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2745 3589 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Camilo Henríquez"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3195 3859h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3195 3859h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-39.81,-73.25&amp;year=1805">What was here in 1805? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
